    <!-- SECTION 1: WHAT IS OSINT -->
    <section id="s1">
      <h2>1. What is OSINT?</h2>
      <p><strong>OSINT</strong> stands for <em>Open-Source Intelligence</em>. In the simplest terms, it is the practice
        of collecting, analyzing, and using information that is <strong>freely and publicly available</strong> — without
        hacking, breaking laws, or accessing private systems.</p>
      <p>Think of it like being the world's most thorough librarian. A librarian doesn't steal books from private homes;
        they work with everything on the public shelves — newspapers, public records, social media posts, satellite
        images, government filings, DNS records, academic papers, and much more.</p>
      <p>The word "open-source" here does not refer to open-source software. It refers to <em>open sources of
          information</em> — data that anyone with internet access can legally find, access, and use.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-title">💡 Analogy</div>
        Imagine you want to find out about a new neighbour. You don't peek through their windows (illegal). Instead, you
        read their LinkedIn profile, check their Twitter posts, look at Google Maps for their street, and find their old
        news article. That's OSINT — legal, ethical, and based on what they publicly shared.
      </div>

      <h3>Key Characteristics of OSINT</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Publicly accessible</strong> — The information is already out there; OSINT just finds it
          efficiently.</li>
        <li><strong>Legal to collect</strong> — No hacking, phishing, or unauthorized access involved.</li>
        <li><strong>Cross-source</strong> — It draws from diverse sources: web, social media, forums, registries,
          satellite imagery, and more.</li>
        <li><strong>Actionable</strong> — The intelligence gathered is used for decisions: security, journalism, law
          enforcement, business, military, etc.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>A Brief History</h3>
      <p>OSINT has existed for centuries. Intelligence agencies have always monitored public newspapers, radio
        broadcasts, and academic literature to gather insights about adversaries. The CIA formally codified OSINT
        practices in the Cold War era.</p>
      <p>With the rise of the internet, OSINT exploded. By the 2000s, the sheer volume of digital footprints — emails,
        websites, forums, social media — made OSINT not just useful but <em>essential</em>. Today, it is a cornerstone
        discipline in cybersecurity, law enforcement, journalism, competitive intelligence, and military operations.</p>
    </section>

