      <h3>Breaking Down Each Phase</h3>
      <h4>Phase 1 — Planning</h4>
      <p>What is the target? What are you trying to find? Define your <em>Key Intelligence Questions (KIQs)</em>.
        Without this, you'll drown in irrelevant data. For example: "Find all publicly-accessible web servers and
        employee email addresses linked to <code>example.com</code>."</p>
      <h4>Phase 2 — Collection</h4>
      <p>This is where OSINT tools shine. Using search engines, specialized databases, APIs, web scrapers, and network
        scanners, you collect raw data about the target — DNS records, social media profiles, job postings, WHOIS data,
        and more.</p>
      <h4>Phase 3 — Processing</h4>
      <p>Raw data is messy. Processing involves <strong>removing duplicates</strong>, tagging sources with confidence
        levels, and structuring the information into something usable — spreadsheets, mind maps, databases.</p>
      <h4>Phase 4 — Analysis</h4>
      <p>Now the real intelligence work begins. You connect the dots — linking people to organizations, mapping
        infrastructure, identifying patterns in behaviour, and spotting anomalies. Link analysis tools (like Maltego)
        are especially useful here.</p>
      <h4>Phase 5 — Reporting</h4>
      <p>Document your findings clearly with evidence, timestamps, and source references. Reports must be reproducible —
        another analyst should be able to verify your conclusions from your notes alone.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- SECTION 4: CATEGORIES -->
    <section id="s4">
      <h2>4. Categories of OSINT Data</h2>
      <p>OSINT draws from an enormous variety of data sources. Understanding the categories helps you pick the right
        tool for each job.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Internet Data</strong> — Websites, blogs, forums, news sites, paste sites (Pastebin), archived pages
          (Wayback Machine).</li>
        <li><strong>Social Media</strong> — Facebook, Twitter/X, Instagram, LinkedIn, Reddit, TikTok posts and profiles.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Public Records</strong> — Government databases, court records, voter registration, business filings,
          patents, land ownership.</li>
        <li><strong>Technical Data</strong> — DNS records, WHOIS registrations, SSL certificates, IP address
          geolocation, exposed APIs, server banners.</li>
        <li><strong>Geospatial Data</strong> — Google Maps, satellite imagery (Sentinel Hub, Planet), geotags in photos,
          GPS metadata.</li>
        <li><strong>Dark Web / Deep Web</strong> — Indexed but hard-to-find forums, Tor hidden services (used cautiously
          and ethically).</li>
        <li><strong>Media &amp; Documents</strong> — PDFs, Word docs, images — all of which can contain hidden
          <em>metadata</em> (author name, GPS coordinates, creation timestamps).</li>
        <li><strong>People Data Aggregators</strong> — Sites like Spokeo, PeopleFinder, Pipl that aggregate personal
          information from multiple public sources.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="callout warn">
        <div class="callout-title">⚠️ Legal Note</div>
        Even though the information is "public," certain collection methods or uses may violate laws like GDPR, CFAA, or
        local privacy statutes. Always understand the legal framework of your jurisdiction before conducting OSINT on
        individuals.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- TOOL 1: MALTEGO -->
    <section id="s5">
      <h2>5. Tool #1 — Maltego</h2>
      <div class="tool-card" data-num="01">
        <div class="tool-name">Maltego</div>
        <div class="tool-tagline">Visual Link Analysis &amp; Intelligence Mapping</div>
        <p>Maltego is widely considered the <strong>most powerful OSINT visualization platform</strong> in existence. It
          was developed by Paterva and is now maintained by Maltego Technologies. At its core, Maltego is a
          <em>graph-based intelligence platform</em> — it lets you map relationships between entities (people,
          organizations, domains, IP addresses, social media accounts, etc.) as a visual network graph.</p>
        <h4>How It Works</h4>
        <p>Maltego works through <strong>Transforms</strong> — automated operations that take one piece of data (an
          <em>entity</em>, like a domain name) and return related data by querying APIs, databases, and the web. For
          example, you enter a domain name, run a transform, and Maltego connects it to IP addresses, email addresses,
          names, social media accounts, and more — all visually linked on a canvas.</p>
        <h4>Step-by-Step: Basic Domain Investigation</h4>
        <ol>
          <li>Open Maltego and create a new graph.</li>
          <li>Drag a <strong>Domain</strong> entity onto the canvas and set it to your target (e.g.,
            <code>example.com</code>).</li>
          <li>Right-click the domain → Run transform → "To DNS Name – SOA" to find the start of authority record.</li>
          <li>Run "To IP Address (DNS)" to map the server's IP.</li>
          <li>From the IP, run "To Location [city]" to geolocate the server.</li>
          <li>Continue expanding — find email addresses, netblocks, WHOIS data, and social links.</li>
        </ol>
        <h4>Real-World Case Study</h4>
        <p>Law enforcement agencies have used Maltego to trace cryptocurrency wallet addresses through a network of
          exchanges, ultimately linking an anonymous wallet to a real-world identity by mapping blockchain transactions,
          forum usernames, and IP registrations into a connected graph.</p>
        <h4>Strengths &amp; Limitations</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>✅ Unmatched visualization for complex relationship mapping</li>
          <li>✅ Huge library of Transforms (400+) from a data marketplace</li>
          <li>✅ Integrates with many third-party data sources</li>
          <li>❌ Steep learning curve for beginners</li>
          <li>❌ Full version (Maltego Pro) is expensive (~$999/year)</li>
          <li>❌ Transform API calls can be slow with large datasets</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="tool-tags">
          <span class="tag">Link Analysis</span>
          <span class="tag">Visualization</span>
          <span class="tag">Network Mapping</span>
          <span class="tag free">Free Tier</span>
          <span class="tag paid">Pro Paid</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- TOOL 2: SHODAN -->
    <section id="s6">
      <h2>6. Tool #2 — Shodan</h2>
      <div class="tool-card" data-num="02">
        <div class="tool-name">Shodan</div>
        <div class="tool-tagline">The Search Engine for Internet-Connected Devices</div>
        <p>Shodan is often called <em>"the scariest search engine on the internet."</em> While Google crawls websites
          looking for text content, <strong>Shodan crawls for devices</strong> — servers, webcams, printers, industrial
          control systems, smart home devices, traffic lights, and anything else connected to the internet.</p>
        <p>Shodan constantly scans the entire IPv4 address space, grabs their <strong>banner information</strong> (the
          data a service sends when you connect to it), and indexes it. The result is a searchable database of every
          exposed internet-facing device on Earth — including many that their owners didn't even know were publicly
          accessible.</p>
        <h4>The Analogy</h4>
        <p>Imagine you could walk through every street in the world and look at what's visible through each window
          without actually entering. You see which buildings have unlocked front doors, which ones are running which
          systems, and which ones have security cameras with default passwords. That's Shodan — a passive reconnaissance
          tool for the internet's infrastructure.</p>
        <h4>What You Can Find</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Exposed webcams (many with no password)</li>
          <li>SCADA/ICS industrial systems (power plants, water treatment)</li>
          <li>Default-credential databases (MongoDB, Elasticsearch)</li>
          <li>SSL certificate details revealing internal infrastructure</li>
          <li>SSH, FTP, Telnet, and RDP services open to the internet</li>
          <li>Router admin panels, VPN endpoints</li>
          <li>Medical devices and hospital systems (alarmingly)</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Using Shodan — Key Search Filters</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><code>hostname:example.com</code> — Find servers in a specific domain</li>
          <li><code>org:"Company Name"</code> — Find servers registered to an organization</li>
          <li><code>port:22 country:IN</code> — Find open SSH servers in India</li>
          <li><code>product:Apache</code> — Find servers running Apache</li>
          <li><code>vuln:CVE-2021-44228</code> — Find servers vulnerable to Log4Shell</li>
          <li><code>default password</code> — (Search within banners) — Find systems with default credentials in their
            banners</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Case Study: Log4Shell Exposure Mapping</h4>
        <p>In December 2021, when the Log4Shell vulnerability was discovered, security teams worldwide used Shodan
          within hours to map which of their internet-facing servers ran vulnerable versions of Log4j. Companies that
          had good Shodan visibility were able to triage faster than those relying solely on internal asset inventories
          — demonstrating that attackers and defenders alike use the same OSINT tools.</p>
        <div class="tool-tags">
          <span class="tag">Device Scanning</span>
          <span class="tag">Network Recon</span>
          <span class="tag">IoT Intelligence</span>
          <span class="tag free">Free Tier</span>
          <span class="tag paid">API Paid</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- TOOL 3: theHarvester -->
    <section id="s7">
      <h2>7. Tool #3 — theHarvester</h2>
      <div class="tool-card" data-num="03">
        <div class="tool-name">theHarvester</div>
        <div class="tool-tagline">Email, Subdomain &amp; Employee Enumeration</div>
        <p><strong>theHarvester</strong> is a command-line tool designed specifically for one purpose: <em>gathering
            email addresses, subdomains, hosts, employee names, open ports, and banners</em> from public sources —
          quickly and efficiently.</p>
        <p>It is a standard component of Kali Linux and is widely used in the early reconnaissance phase of penetration
          testing. Think of it as a high-speed public data harvester that queries dozens of search engines and public
          databases simultaneously.</p>
        <h4>Data Sources theHarvester Queries</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Google, Bing, Yahoo, DuckDuckGo (web search results)</li>
          <li>LinkedIn (employee names and titles)</li>
          <li>Shodan (exposed services)</li>
          <li>DNSdumpster (DNS records)</li>
          <li>CertSpotter / crt.sh (SSL certificate transparency logs)</li>
          <li>Hunter.io (email pattern discovery)</li>
          <li>Twitter, GitHub, and more</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Basic Command Syntax</h4>
        <p>Running theHarvester against a domain:</p>
        <p><code>theHarvester -d example.com -b google,linkedin,shodan -l 500</code></p>
        <p>Where: <code>-d</code> = target domain, <code>-b</code> = data sources, <code>-l</code> = result limit. The
          output typically reveals employee email addresses (which follow predictable patterns), subdomains of the
          target, and IP address ranges.</p>
        <h4>Why Email Enumeration Matters</h4>
        <p>Once you have a list of real employee email addresses, you can: verify the email naming convention (e.g.,
          <code>firstname.lastname@company.com</code>), check if any appear in breach databases (using HaveIBeenPwned),
          perform targeted spear-phishing simulations (in authorized pen tests), and map the org structure from LinkedIn
          job titles.</p>
        <div class="tool-tags">
          <span class="tag">Email Harvesting</span>
          <span class="tag">Subdomain Discovery</span>
          <span class="tag">Recon</span>
          <span class="tag free">Free &amp; Open Source</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- TOOL 4: RECON-NG -->
    <section id="s8">
      <h2>8. Tool #4 — Recon-ng</h2>
      <div class="tool-card" data-num="04">
        <div class="tool-name">Recon-ng</div>
        <div class="tool-tagline">Modular Web Reconnaissance Framework</div>
        <p>Recon-ng is a full-featured web reconnaissance framework written in Python. Its design philosophy mirrors
          Metasploit — a modular, extensible platform where each module performs a specific intelligence-gathering task.
          If you are comfortable with Metasploit's console, you will feel immediately at home in Recon-ng.</p>
        <p>It is especially powerful for <strong>automating large-scale, multi-source reconnaissance</strong> — querying
          dozens of data sources in sequence, storing results in a built-in SQLite database, and generating professional
          reports.</p>
        <h4>Architecture: Workspaces &amp; Modules</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Workspaces</strong> — Each investigation gets its own workspace, keeping data from different
            targets isolated.</li>
          <li><strong>Modules</strong> — There are modules for WHOIS lookup, DNS brute-forcing, social media profiling,
            credential checking, geolocation, and more. Each module is installed independently from the Recon-ng
            marketplace.</li>
          <li><strong>Database</strong> — All results are stored in a relational database, making it easy to query:
            "Give me all email addresses found during this investigation."</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Sample Workflow</h4>
        <ol>
          <li>Launch: <code>recon-ng</code></li>
          <li>Create workspace: <code>workspaces create target_company</code></li>
          <li>Add a seed domain: <code>db insert domains</code></li>
          <li>Install modules: <code>marketplace install recon/domains-hosts/hackertarget</code></li>
          <li>Run module to discover subdomains from HackerTarget API</li>
          <li>Chain with DNS resolution module to get IP addresses</li>
          <li>Export report: <code>reporting/html</code></li>
        </ol>
        <div class="callout success">
          <div class="callout-title">✅ Pro Tip</div>
          Recon-ng's database allows you to run SQL queries directly:
          <code>SELECT DISTINCT email FROM contacts WHERE email LIKE '%@target.com'</code> — very useful when you have
          accumulated thousands of records across multiple modules.
        </div>
        <div class="tool-tags">
          <span class="tag">Framework</span>
          <span class="tag">Automated Recon</span>
          <span class="tag">Modular</span>
          <span class="tag free">Free &amp; Open Source</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- TOOL 5: SPIDERFOOT -->
    <section id="s9">
      <h2>9. Tool #5 — SpiderFoot</h2>
      <div class="tool-card" data-num="05">
        <div class="tool-name">SpiderFoot</div>
        <div class="tool-tagline">Automated OSINT Intelligence Platform</div>
        <p>SpiderFoot is an <strong>automated OSINT collection platform</strong> that can gather intelligence on IP
          addresses, domains, email addresses, usernames, phone numbers, and more — all from a single input. It has a
          polished web-based interface and can run hundreds of modules simultaneously, making it one of the most
          comprehensive automated OSINT tools available.</p>
        <p>SpiderFoot HX is the cloud-hosted commercial version, while the open-source version is freely available on
          GitHub and can be self-hosted.</p>
        <h4>What It Covers</h4>
        <p>SpiderFoot integrates with <strong>200+ data sources</strong>, including Shodan, VirusTotal, HaveIBeenPwned,
          ThreatCrowd, PasteBin, BGP route data, dark web monitoring, and dozens more. When you enter a target, it
          automatically fans out across all these sources, correlates the results, and presents a rich profile — all
          without manual queries.</p>
        <h4>Use Case: Brand Exposure Assessment</h4>
        <p>A company's security team runs SpiderFoot against their own domain. Within minutes, it surfaces: three
          subdomains not in their asset inventory, two email addresses appearing in breach databases, an open port on a
          forgotten test server, and several employee usernames linked across public forums. This is what the attackers
          see — and now the defenders do too.</p>
