    <!-- TOOL 7: OSINT FRAMEWORK -->
    <section id="s11">
      <h2>11. Tool #7 — OSINT Framework</h2>
      <div class="tool-card" data-num="07">
        <div class="tool-name">OSINT Framework</div>
        <div class="tool-tagline">The Master Directory of OSINT Resources</div>
        <p>OSINT Framework (<code>osintframework.com</code>) is not a scanning or harvesting tool — it is a
          <strong>curated, categorized directory</strong> of hundreds of OSINT tools and resources, organized as an
          interactive mind map. Think of it as the OSINT universe's master index, built and maintained by Justin
          Nordine.</p>
        <h4>How to Use It</h4>
        <p>The framework is organized as a tree starting from "OSINT Framework." You drill down through categories —
          Username, Email Address, Domain Name, IP Address, Business Records, Transportation, and more — until you reach
          the specific tool or resource that fits your need. Each leaf node links directly to the relevant tool or
          database.</p>
        <h4>Categories Include</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Username search tools (Namechk, Sherlock, WhatsMyName)</li>
          <li>Email investigation (Hunter.io, EmailRep, HaveIBeenPwned)</li>
          <li>IP address tools (Shodan, Censys, ipinfo.io)</li>
          <li>Social network analysis tools</li>
          <li>Dark web search engines</li>
          <li>Vehicle and license plate registries</li>
          <li>Geolocation and mapping tools</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="callout">
          <div class="callout-title">💡 Best Practice</div>
          Bookmark OSINT Framework before starting any investigation. When you don't know <em>which</em> tool to use,
          it's the fastest way to find the right resource for your specific data type — no searching, no guessing.
        </div>
