    <!-- TOOL 9: CREEPY -->
    <section id="s13">
      <h2>13. Tool #9 — Creepy</h2>
      <div class="tool-card" data-num="09">
        <div class="tool-name">Creepy</div>
        <div class="tool-tagline">Geolocation Intelligence from Social Media</div>
        <p>Creepy is an <strong>open-source geolocation OSINT tool</strong> that extracts location information from
          photos posted on social media platforms. Its name is intentional — and it highlights exactly why geolocation
          tracking is such a significant privacy concern.</p>
        <h4>How It Works</h4>
        <p>When people upload photos to social media, many platforms preserve the photo's <strong>EXIF metadata</strong>
          — including GPS coordinates embedded by smartphones. Creepy collects these geotagged posts, plots them on a
          map, and builds a <em>movement timeline</em> for the target — showing where they were, at what time, on what
          date, extrapolated from their own public posts.</p>
        <h4>Real-World Privacy Implications</h4>
        <p>In multiple cases, journalists and law enforcement have used geolocation OSINT to locate missing persons,
          identify the location of hostage videos (Bellingcat's open-source investigations), and track terrorist
          suspects' movement patterns based purely on geotagged social media photos. The technique, called
          <strong>geolocation verification</strong>, has become a discipline of its own in investigative journalism.</p>
        <div class="callout warn">
          <div class="callout-title">⚠️ Ethical Caution</div>
          Geolocation tracking of individuals raises serious ethical and legal concerns. This tool must only be used for
          lawful, authorized purposes — such as tracking your own digital footprint, authorized investigations, or
          journalistic public-interest work. Tracking private individuals without consent is illegal in most
          jurisdictions.
        </div>
        <div class="tool-tags">
          <span class="tag">Geolocation</span>
          <span class="tag">EXIF Data</span>
          <span class="tag">Social Media</span>
          <span class="tag free">Free &amp; Open Source</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- TOOL 10: GOOGLE DORKS -->
    <section id="s14">
      <h2>14. Tool #10 — Google Dorks (Google Hacking)</h2>
      <div class="tool-card" data-num="10">
        <div class="tool-name">Google Dorks</div>
        <div class="tool-tagline">Advanced Search Operators for Precision Intelligence</div>
        <p><strong>Google Dorking</strong> (also called Google Hacking) uses Google's advanced search operators to find
          specific, sensitive, or hard-to-find information that is publicly indexed but not easily discovered through
          standard searches. It requires no tools beyond a web browser — just knowledge of Google's powerful filter
          syntax.</p>
        <p>The technique is named after Johnny Long, who popularized it through his "Google Hacking Database" (GHDB).
          The GHDB contains thousands of pre-built dork queries for finding everything from exposed admin panels to
          login credentials accidentally uploaded to GitHub.</p>
        <h4>Essential Google Dork Operators</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><code>site:example.com</code> — Restrict results to a specific domain</li>
          <li><code>filetype:pdf</code> — Find specific file types</li>
          <li><code>intitle:"index of"</code> — Find open directory listings</li>
          <li><code>inurl:admin</code> — Find URLs containing "admin"</li>
          <li><code>intext:"password"</code> — Find pages containing specific text</li>
          <li><code>"@example.com" filetype:xls</code> — Find Excel files with email addresses from a domain</li>
          <li><code>site:github.com "example.com" "password"</code> — Find accidentally committed credentials on GitHub
          </li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Powerful Combination Examples</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><code>site:example.com filetype:pdf intitle:"confidential"</code> — PDFs marked confidential on a domain
          </li>
          <li><code>inurl:/wp-admin site:example.com</code> — WordPress admin login pages</li>
          <li><code>site:pastebin.com "example.com" "password"</code> — Check if credentials leaked to Pastebin</li>
          <li><code>intitle:"webcamXP" inurl:8080</code> — Find exposed webcams on port 8080</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>The Google Hacking Database (GHDB)</h4>
        <p>The GHDB (maintained at exploit-db.com/google-hacking-database) is a searchable library of thousands of
          proven Google dork queries, categorized by what they expose: files containing passwords, sensitive
          directories, vulnerable servers, misconfigured web applications, and more. Any OSINT investigator should be
          familiar with this resource.</p>
        <div class="tool-tags">
          <span class="tag">Search Intelligence</span>
          <span class="tag">No Install Needed</span>
          <span class="tag">Passive Recon</span>
