      <h3>Core Principles for Responsible OSINT</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Legality first</strong> — Check local laws. GDPR in Europe, CFAA in the US, and IT Act in India all
          have implications for data collection.</li>
        <li><strong>Purpose limitation</strong> — Collect only what you need for the stated purpose. Don't build
          profiles "just in case."</li>
        <li><strong>No active exploitation</strong> — Finding a vulnerability via Shodan is OSINT. Logging into it with
          a default password is a crime.</li>
        <li><strong>Respect private individuals</strong> — Public figures and organizations have lower privacy
          expectations than private citizens. Treat the latter with extreme care.</li>
        <li><strong>Document everything</strong> — Keep timestamped notes of what you collected, from where, and why.
          This protects you legally and makes your work reproducible.</li>
        <li><strong>Use the minimum necessary</strong> — The principle of data minimization: don't collect more than
          your investigation requires.</li>
      </ul>
